If you have looked for a job at a large company, you have probably filled in a Workday
form. It is the hiring system behind more open postings than any other we crawl:
**842,756** of them right now, at **5,424** employers. This is the first in a series on
what the big hiring systems look like from the job seeker's side, and it starts with the
biggest.

## "Posted 30+ Days Ago" is most of the board

A Workday career site does not show the date a job was posted. It shows "Posted Today",
"Posted 3 Days Ago", and after a month, "Posted 30+ Days Ago" — whether the posting is
five weeks old or two years. Behind that label, Workday's own data carries the real date,
and we keep it.

| open Workday postings older than | share |
| --- | --- |
| 30 days | 66.8% |
| 60 days | 43.9% |
| 90 days | 31.8% |
| 6 months | 14.3% |
| a year | 5.3% |

**Two in three** open Workday postings are past the point where the label stops counting.
The median one is **50 days** old; across all the other sources we can date, it is 40, and
59% are past 30 days. One Workday posting in twenty has been up for more than a year, and
on the career site it looks the same as one posted five weeks ago.

## How long a Workday posting stays up

Of the **1,295,156** Workday postings we saw come down since June, half were gone within
**42 days**. A quarter came down within 20 days, three in four within 76. Only 5% closed in
their first week, and 19% stayed up three months or more.

That is slower than the market: across all sources, half of closed postings were gone
within 32 days ([more on that here](/blog/2026-11-18-how-long-job-postings-really-stay-up)). Workday
is used mostly by large employers, and they tend to hire slowly: more approvals, more
interview rounds, and more postings kept open to fill the same role many times over.

Those two numbers explain each other. A posting that closes in a month is on the board for a
month; one that stays up for a year is there every day of that year. So the board fills up
with old postings, and a 42-day median to close becomes a board where two thirds of what you
see is past the 30-day mark.

## What a Workday posting tells you, and what it does not

Workday gives every employer the same fields, but several are optional, and many
employers leave them empty.

- **The date.** Always there in the data: every open Workday posting we hold has one. The
  career site just rounds it off.
- **Remote or not.** Workday has a field for it, yet for **84%** of open postings neither
  that field nor the description gave us an answer. Of the rest, hybrid is the most common answer (7.5% of all postings),
  then on-site (5.0%) and remote (3.9%). For the rest, the answer is somewhere in the
  description, or nowhere.
- **Full or part time.** Workday's own field only knows those two. Contracts, internships
  and fixed-term roles have to be read out of the title or the text: 9% of open postings are
  contracts and 3% internships by our reading, and for 39% we could not tell.
- **Pay.** The posting data Workday career sites give out has no pay field. Where there is a
  range, it is in the description. In the United States **35%** of open Workday postings
  name a dollar figure there, against 22% for everything else we crawl — and where the law
  asks for it, about half do.

| state | Workday postings with a dollar figure |
| --- | --- |
| New York | 59.5% |
| Massachusetts | 52.5% |
| Illinois | 51.7% |
| California | 50.1% |
| Texas | 28.7% |
| Ohio | 23.6% |
| Pennsylvania | 20.9% |
| Florida | 20.6% |

The first four require a pay range in job postings; the last four do not. Even under the
law, half the postings show no dollar figure. Some state the range in another form, and
some fall outside a law that covers only employers above a certain size; we cannot tell
those apart from the ones that simply leave pay out.

## When Workday postings go up

Workday postings follow the working week more strictly than most: Tuesday gets 20.7% of
them, Wednesday 20.3%, Friday 17.6%, and each weekend day about 1%, against 3% across the
market. Tuesday is the busiest day across the market too ([the full breakdown](/blog/2026-11-19-which-day-are-jobs-posted)).

## What it means for your search

- **Treat "30+ Days Ago" as "unknown, probably much older".** Of the postings with that
  label, two in three are more than two months old, and one in five more than six months.
- **Find the real date before you spend an hour on the form.** freehire shows the date the
  employer gave the posting, so a Workday job posted 14 months ago says so.
- **Apply early, but do not rule out an old posting.** Postings at large employers stay
  open for a long time, and some of the old ones are roles that hire continuously. An old
  posting is worth a check, not an automatic skip.
- **Expect one account per employer.** Each company's Workday site is separate, so applying
  to five companies means five sign-ups. A password manager saves a lot of time.

## How we counted

Open postings are those live on employers' Workday career sites as of 21 November 2026,
counted once each, from public career sites only. The posting date is the start date
Workday gives for the posting. Pay is counted from a sample of open US postings: a posting
counts if its description names a dollar amount, so a sign-on bonus counts too, and states
are read from the location where it names one. Closed postings are those that disappeared from the
employer's site between mid-June, when we started watching, and mid-November; the time is
measured from the posting date to the day it disappeared, and postings we stopped tracking
for our own reasons (a board we could not reach, for example) are left out. A posting
coming down does not mean someone was hired.
